                <pb facs="0693" n="579" sameAs="frus:frus1924v01/pg579"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.74/499: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Mayer</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1924-11-29T11:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">November 29,
                                1924—11 a.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received November 29—6:43
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>457. Legation’s despatch 2514, September 23 [<hi rendition="#italic">20</hi>],
                        1924.<anchor n="12" xml:id="d558-f1"/> Yeh Kung-ch’o who signed original
                    Federal radio contract has assumed office as Minister of Communications and
                    Chiang Tsun-wei [<hi rendition="#italic">Chiang Tsim-i?</hi>], former Director
                    General of Telegraphs, is also to be reappointed <pb facs="0694" n="580"
                        xml:id="pg580"/> to that post. I am led to believe they will shortly reopen
                    negotiations with a view to executing this contract and I respectfully suggest
                    Department ascertain at once whether American parties prepared to proceed and
                    how negotiations are to be conducted. If Federal Company has not abandoned plan
                    I consider it most desirable that representative with full powers should come to
                    Peking immediately.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Mayer</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
